00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:06.850
Switching topics though off of Donald Trump,
let's get into something a little more meaningful,

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:07.930
right?

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:19.110
Well, according to New Poles, 70% of Republican
voters here in the United States, vast majority,

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:26.830
70% actually support the idea put forward
last week by Senator Bernie Sanders and representative

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:33.190
Alexandria Kazuyo to cap interest rates for
credit cards and payday lenders.

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:44.170
At fifth teen percent, 70% of Republicans
love this idea put forward by these democratic

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:46.809
socialists.

00:00:46.809 --> 00:00:52.389
The reason I love this story is because right
now you have certain Democratic presidential

00:00:52.389 --> 00:00:57.889
candidates named Joe Biden who are telling
us that we can't move too far to the left,

00:00:57.889 --> 00:01:00.719
that you're gonna scare off the moderate Republicans.

00:01:00.719 --> 00:01:05.650
You're not going to get any republicans to
your side pushing these far left crazy anti

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:07.539
corporate ideas.

00:01:07.539 --> 00:01:09.250
That's what the establishment tells us.

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:13.240
That's what the conventional wisdom in DC
tries to tell us.

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:19.540
But it turns out that time after time in poll
after poll, Republicans actually really love

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:24.960
the socialist ideas and it's not just the
capping of the credit card interest rates,

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:30.950
which as of last week, we're at a almost 22%
average.

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:35.429
People with good credit are still paying more
than 15% on credit cards.

00:01:35.429 --> 00:01:39.770
People with very poor or no credit who have
to go to payday lenders.

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:43.719
You're talking about dozens upon dozens of
percentage points in interest.

00:01:43.719 --> 00:01:50.990
I mean 30 40 50 or more in interest on those
just because they had no credit.

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:54.710
They get trapped in these endless debt cycles
for this $9 billion industry.

00:01:54.710 --> 00:02:00.200
In fact, that $9 billion they make has only
off the interest each year and they're doing

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:04.920
better than ever because Trump has gutted
the CFPB and now they're not regulating these

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.869
people like they had been towards the end
of the Obama administration.

00:02:08.869 --> 00:02:09.940
So they're back.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:15.440
The predatory payday lenders are back and
they're screwing over American citizens, and

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:20.940
70% of Republicans understand that just like
70% of Republicans understand that credit

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:25.610
card companies are doing the exact same thing.

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:29.860
But it's not just capping the interest rates
here.

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:32.000
They support Medicare for all.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:34.480
They support a $15 minimum wage.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:38.610
They support getting money out of politics
and overturning citizens United.

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:42.140
They support taking action on climate change.

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:49.290
They support renewable energy, they support
debt free or free college.

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:56.480
They support all of these things that have
been trashed as far left socialist ideas.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:01.140
Now to throw a wrench in the works, there's
another pole from Gallup that says that only

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:05.650
40% of Americans say that socialism would
be good for the United States.

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:12.890
51% say that it would be bad, but the flaw
with this Gallup poll is that it's straight

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:18.010
up just asking people what socialism be a
good thing or a bad thing for the United States.

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:23.980
Once you start breaking that down into individual
policies, that might be socialism, but if

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:26.170
you just say, Hey, what about Medicare for
all?

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:28.010
Do you that guess what?

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:30.120
Overwhelming majority of everybody says, yes,
I do.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.450
I think that's a good idea.

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:35.760
Even if you mention afterwards that well,
isn't that kind of socialism?

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:38.430
They'll say, oh, well, I guess it is, but
I like it.

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:42.680
Same thing with social security, which also,
by the way, a majority of all parties support

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:45.100
expanding social security.

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:49.980
We, we have socialism in place in the United
States today already.

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:53.920
So this Gallup poll that says only 40% think
it would be a good thing.

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:54.920
Yeah.

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:59.250
When you ask a misleading question, when you
ask about a word, which is socialism, that

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:03.480
most people, honest to God, couldn't even
define, then yeah, they're probably going

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:05.310
to say it's bad.

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:11.210
But once you start breaking it down into individual
policies, once you divorce those policies

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:15.740
from a politician or political party, support
for them goes up.

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:20.680
Other polls have proven that when you attach
it to a political party, the support does

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:26.320
drop a little, not a ton, but it does drop
a little, but it shows you that the American

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:33.790
public is hungry for policy, and more importantly,
they're hungry for policies that most Republicans

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:34.790
and even some

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:39.460
centrist Democrats would absolutely classify
as socially.

